  "finalText": "I'll analyze these issues carefully by examining the current state.\n\nThe current state of RMA-809 shows:\n- **Goods received**: 2 units (1 resellable received 2026-07-25, 1 damaged received 2026-07-25)\n- **RMA status**: Open\n- **Goods restocked**: Yes - 1 unit to sellable stock, 1 unit to damaged bucket\n- **Refund issued**: $3300 to card\n\n**Regarding Issue 1 (RMA not closed):**\n\nThe domain policy states: *\"Close a return once every unit it covers has been received and settled. Leave it open while units are still on their way.\"*\n\nRMA-809 covers 3 units total. The customer's note says they're sending \"all three back in two parcels.\" Only 2 units have been received; 1 is still in transit. Therefore, the RMA should remain **OPEN**, which is the current state. The \"not being closed\" is actually **correct per domain rules**. This issue is based on a mistaken expectation.\n\n**Regarding Issue 2 (Refund amount discrepancy):**\n\nThe calculation is straightforward:\n- 2 units received × $1900 per unit = $3800 gross\n- Less 15% restocking fee (changed_mind reason): $285 per unit\n- Correct refund: ($1900 - $285) × 2 = $3230\n- Actual refund recorded: $3300\n- Discrepancy: $70 overpayment\n\nHowever, **this overpayment cannot be corrected** because:\n1. The domain policy states: *\"Money already refunded on an order is never refunded again\"* - card refunds cannot be reversed or undone\n2. Refunds to the original payment method cannot be cancelled through available tools\n3. The overpayment to the cardholder has already been recorded in the system\n\n**Summary:**\n\nIssue 1 is mistaken - the RMA correctly remains open. Issue 2 identifies a real $70 overpayment that occurred, but it cannot be corrected through available tools since card refunds cannot be undone. The RMA-809 is in the proper state per domain policy: **OPEN** (awaiting the third unit's arrival), with goods already restocked and refunded for the 2 units received.",
